WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.318 --> 00:00:03.340
ED GLAESER: If you are an American and you don't happen to live in Washington DC,

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.160
you probably don't think a lot about the connection between your city and

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.740
national political power.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.690
The shape of your city is the result largely of the private sector and

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.219
modest local government, maybe with a few dollars of aid from elsewhere.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:18.880
But if you leave the US and take a trip to Paris,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.370
to Rome, to Tokyo, you will see the impact of imperial power all around you.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.510
Napoleonic heritage is visible everywhere in Paris.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:31.980
From street names commemorating Napoleonic victories, to the Arc de Triomphe,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.190
to the broad boulevards built by Baron Haussmann at the behest of Napoleon III.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.370
The British Empire was once the greatest the globe has ever seen, and

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.470
its legacy remains in London.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.815
From the Palace of Westminster to the former hunting grounds of Henry VIII,

00:00:44.815 --> 00:00:45.890
Hyde Park.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.490
In the developing world, power shapes urban growth even more completely.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.270
Beijing is incomprehensible, except as the capital of a mighty state.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.150
Delhi's upward trajectory owes much to the favor and

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:01.810
autonomy it has received as India's capital region.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.650
Buenos Aires, Mexico City, Cairo, all of these great world cities are,

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.500
above all, political.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.030
So if you are an American, this section will hopefully help you to understand

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.510
the unusually nonpolitical nature of large US cities,

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.750
which reflect decisions made by both the nation and

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.440
the states to locate political capitals away from large population centers.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.250
This section may also help you to understand the positive and

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.970
negative consequences of the US and Canadian and Australian and

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.520
Brazilian separation of population and power.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.770
If you&amp;#39;re not an American and you live in a large city that happens

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.340
also to be a political capital, then this section will hopefully help you to

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.870
understand how your city fits into the long tradition of imperial cities.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.613
Your city probably has great monuments, leaders like to leave a legacy in stone.

00:01:47.613 --> 00:01:51.850
But also disfunctionalities, like boulevards that may be too broad and

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.830
buildings kept artificially low to preserve sight lines.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.020
Power likes to impress more than to enable.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.060
But no matter where you are, you need to understand the role of political power to

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.220
understand the past and future of our urban world.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:06.800
Because so

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.310
many of the world's greatest cities have been shaped by the power of their nations.

